\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces  A comparison of the merger rate at redshift $z=0$, as a function of component mass for the \textsc  {FullPop}-4.0\xspace  and \ac {BGP} models. In the upper panel, we show the merger rate as a function of primary mass, marginalized over secondary mass. In the lower panel, we show the merger rate as a function of the secondary mass, marginalized over the primary mass. At $m_2 = 5\,\,M_\odot $, the \textsc  {FullPop}-4.0\xspace  model transitions to an alternative pairing function to allow for \acp {NSBH}, hence the discontinuity in the secondary mass. The median of the inferred merger rate is shown with a solid line, and the 90\% credible interval is shown in the shaded region. The \ac {BGP} weakly modeled approach\xspace  has larger uncertainties due to increased flexibility. Both the strongly modeled\xspace  and weakly modeled\xspace  approaches find local maxima in the merger rate at $m_2 \sim 1-2 \,\,M_\odot $ and $m_2 \sim 6.5-9\,\,M_\odot $ in the secondary-mass distribution. However, the \ac {BGP} model does not confidently recover the low mass peaks in the primary-mass distribution. In the inset, we show the \textsc  {FullPop}-4.0\xspace  inferred gap depth parameter $A$ (see Appendix~\ref {appendix:PDB}), where $A=1$ (a completely empty gap) is disfavored. }}{264}{figure.caption.148}\protected@file@percent }
\acronymused{BGP}
\acronymused{NSBH}
\acronymused{BGP}
\acronymused{BGP}
\newlabel{fig:full_mass_spectrum}{{7.2}{264}{A comparison of the merger rate at redshift $z=0$, as a function of component mass for the \PDB and \ac {BGP} models. In the upper panel, we show the merger rate as a function of primary mass, marginalized over secondary mass. In the lower panel, we show the merger rate as a function of the secondary mass, marginalized over the primary mass. At $m_2 = 5\,\Msun $, the \PDB model transitions to an alternative pairing function to allow for \acp {NSBH}, hence the discontinuity in the secondary mass. The median of the inferred merger rate is shown with a solid line, and the 90\% credible interval is shown in the shaded region. The \ac {BGP} \nonparametric has larger uncertainties due to increased flexibility. Both the \parametricAdj and \nonparametricAdj approaches find local maxima in the merger rate at $m_2 \sim 1-2 \,\Msun $ and $m_2 \sim 6.5-9\,\Msun $ in the secondary-mass distribution. However, the \ac {BGP} model does not confidently recover the low mass peaks in the primary-mass distribution. In the inset, we show the \PDB inferred gap depth parameter $A$ (see Appendix~\ref {appendix:PDB}), where $A=1$ (a completely empty gap) is disfavored}{figure.caption.148}{}}
